<Base
  title="About"
  description="Abhinav Gupta is a full-stack software engineer building backend platforms and browser tooling, with security experience. Two years at SquareX and Zscaler, now looking at architecture roles."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="label"><b>About</b> / The longer version</p>
    <h1>Hi, I’m <em class="hl">Abhinav</em>.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I’m a full-stack engineer who ended up living inside a browser. For 2
      years I worked on one built to keep company data on company machines, and
      I had my hands in most of its layers. Next I’d like to design systems like
      that from the first box.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="prose cv">
    <h2>Zscaler <span class="meta">Feb 2026 – Jul 2026</span></h2>
    <div>
      <p>
        Zscaler bought SquareX, and I came along with the furniture. I was on the
        founding team of the Zero Trust Browser, so mostly it meant more
        customers and a lot more Chromium.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Wrote 20+ native extension APIs in C++ and Objective-C++ that read a laptop’s health: antivirus, disk encryption, MDM enrollment, certificates. 17 of them landed in 11 days. Together they expose 50+ signals, so the browser can check a device without a separate agent.</li>
        <li>Rewrote the page masking that hides card numbers and personal data on any site, Google Docs included. The old version re-indexed the whole page every 500 ms. The new one only touches what changed, and it does 2× the work with about 30% less memory per tab.</li>
        <li>Wrote the first commit of our Android browser, a Firefox fork, and designed the build tooling 3 other engineers then built on. I moved the whole security extension from desktop to Android myself.</li>
        <li>Built download-to-cloud. When policy says so, your download goes to the company’s Google Drive, OneDrive or Box, and the local copy never lands.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>SquareX <span class="meta">Jun 2024 – Feb 2026</span></h2>
    <div>
      <p>
        I joined as a summer intern and kept showing up. The platform ran for 80+
        companies and 40,000+ people at once, which made every bug feel
        personal.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Owned the Go identity provider that Entra ID and Okta call when you sign in. If you aren’t on the Zero Trust Browser, you don’t get in. Later I rewrote most of it, so tenant config now hot-reloads over Kafka with no restarts.</li>
        <li>Made 631 commits to the React admin console over 18 months. The biggest piece was a deployment-script generator: pick 1 of 8 MDMs, an OS and a browser, and it writes the install script for you.</li>
        <li>Added TOTP two-factor sign-in, and put the product docs (public until then) behind a login.</li>
        <li>As an intern, co-built <a href="https://browser.security">browser.security</a>. I wrote 29 of its 46 ways to sneak a file past a web security gateway (split over WebRTC, hidden inside images, that sort of thing) and the demo site. SquareX took it to DEF CON 32, and Forbes covered it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>On the side</h2>
    <div>
      <p>
        Apps, libraries and the odd research project. The ones with an
        interesting decision get a drawing <a href="/#builds-h">on the home page</a>,
        and the rest are on <a href="/projects">the projects page</a>. I keep <a href="/dsa">notes on every DSA problem I solve</a>, and the <a href="/lab">lab</a> has
        2 small games about caches and latency.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Tools</h2>
    <div>
      <p>
        Mostly C++, Go, TypeScript and Python, with Rust and CUDA when a side
        project asks for them. Kafka, Redis, Postgres, GCP and Docker at work. I
        know more about Chromium and Firefox internals than is strictly healthy.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>School</h2>
    <div>
      <p>
        B.Tech in Computer Science, IIT Tirupati, 2021–2025. I was also elected
        Students’ General Secretary, and we finished that year net-zero on a
        budget that had been cut 25%. Codeforces Expert and LeetCode Knight,
        when the contests go my way.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
